# Custom Lovelace Card Guidelines

Guidelines for building custom Home Assistant Lovelace cards that work across all target devices:

- **Desktop**: Chrome/Firefox/Edge on Windows/Mac/Linux
- **iOS**: Home Assistant Companion App (iPhone, iPad)
- **Android**: Home Assistant Companion App, UniFi Connect Display (wall-mounted touchscreen)

## 1) Communication: relay script pattern only

Cards **must not** use `fire_event`, `callApi("POST", "events/...")`, or `connection.sendMessagePromise({ type: "fire_event" })`. These require **admin privileges** and fail silently on non-admin device accounts.

Use `hass.callService()` exclusively — it works for all authenticated users:

```javascript
_callRelay(command, data) {
  if (!this._hass) return;
  this._hass.callService("script", "<app>_relay", {
    command,
    payload: JSON.stringify(data || {}),
  }).catch((err) => {
    console.warn("<card>: relay failed", command, err);
  });
}
```

See `appdaemon-architecture.mdc` §3 for the full relay script pattern.

## 2) Touch + click event delegation

Use a single delegated event system on the shadow root. Mark interactive elements with `data-action` attributes and route through a central `_dispatchAction(action, el)` handler.

### The problem

Touch devices fire `touchstart` → `touchend` → `click` in sequence. Without deduplication, actions fire twice. Android webviews (especially UniFi Connect) are stricter about `preventDefault()` blocking native form behavior than iOS or desktop.

### Required pattern

```javascript
_ensureDelegatedListeners() {
  if (this._delegatedBound) return;
  this._delegatedBound = true;

  const root = this.shadowRoot;
  let touchActive = false;
  let touchCancelled = false;

  // Cancel touch on scroll/move
  ["touchcancel", "touchmove", "scroll"].forEach((evt) => {
    root.addEventListener(evt, () => { touchCancelled = true; }, { passive: true });
  });

  root.addEventListener("touchstart", () => {
    touchActive = true;
    touchCancelled = false;
  }, { passive: true });

  root.addEventListener("touchend", (e) => {
    const el = findActionTarget(e);
    if (touchCancelled || !el) { touchActive = false; return; }

    // CRITICAL: never preventDefault on native form elements — Android
    // webviews will not open keyboards, dropdowns, or date pickers.
    const tag = el.tagName?.toLowerCase();
    const nativeEl = tag === "input" || tag === "select" || tag === "textarea";
    if (!nativeEl && e.cancelable) e.preventDefault();

    dispatchAction(el);
    setTimeout(() => { touchActive = false; }, 400);
  });

  // Desktop click — skip if touch just handled it
  root.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
    if (touchActive) return;
    const el = findActionTarget(e);
    if (el) dispatchAction(el);
  });
}
```

### Key rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| **Never** `preventDefault()` on `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` touchend | Android webviews won't open keyboard or dropdown |
| Use `{ passive: true }` for `touchstart`, `touchmove`, `touchcancel`, `scroll` | Required by modern browsers; improves scroll performance |
| Gate click handler with `if (touchActive) return` | Prevents double-fire on touch devices |
| Use 300–400ms timeout to clear `touchActive` | Covers the touch→click delay on all platforms |
| Use `e.composedPath()` to find `data-action` target | Works correctly inside shadow DOM |

## 3) Form inputs must not lose focus on re-render

When the card re-renders (e.g., after `_markDirty()`), the entire shadow DOM is replaced, stealing focus from any active input field. This makes typing impossible — each keystroke triggers a re-render that deselects the field.

### Fix

Before re-rendering, check if the active element is an input. If so, skip the render or defer it:

```javascript
_render() {
  const active = this.shadowRoot?.activeElement;
  if (active && (active.tagName === "INPUT" || active.tagName === "TEXTAREA")) {
    // Don't re-render while user is typing — the save button is already enabled
    return;
  }
  // ... actual render logic
}
```

For date inputs (`<input type="date">`), also skip re-render since the date picker counts as an active input on mobile.

## 4) Structural changes require explicit save

Actions that modify the card's data model (add, remove, reorder items) should call `_markDirty()` and re-render — **not** auto-save. The user must click "Save" to persist changes. This allows discarding unintentional actions (e.g., accidental delete).

Auto-save is only appropriate for actions that are explicitly confirmatory (the Save button itself).

## 5) Cache busting

Custom cards served from `/local/` or `/config/www/` are aggressively cached by browsers and HA companion apps. After updating a card's JS file on the HA server:

1. Update the Lovelace resource URL's query parameter: `?v=N` → `?v=N+1`
2. Users must hard-refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R on desktop, pull-to-refresh on mobile)

Use the MCP `ha_config_set_dashboard_resource` tool to bump the version:

```
resource_id: "<id from ha_config_list_dashboard_resources>"
url: "/local/path/to/card.js?v=<N+1>"
```

## 6) Shadow DOM and styling

- All card HTML lives inside `this.shadowRoot` (via `attachShadow({ mode: "open" })`)
- Styles are scoped to the shadow DOM — use `<style>` tags inside the shadow root
- HA theme CSS variables (e.g., `var(--primary-color)`, `var(--card-background-color)`) are inherited into shadow DOM and should be used for consistent theming
- External stylesheets and global CSS do not penetrate the shadow boundary

## 7) Card structure

Every custom card must:

1. Extend `HTMLElement`
2. Define `setConfig(config)` — called once with card YAML config
3. Define `set hass(hass)` — called on every HA state change; use to read sensor data and trigger renders
4. Register with `customElements.define("<card-name>", CardClass)`
5. Optionally define `getCardSize()` — returns estimated card height in grid units

### Minimal skeleton

```javascript
class MyCard extends HTMLElement {
  setConfig(config) {
    this._config = config;
    if (!this.shadowRoot) {
      this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
    }
  }

  set hass(hass) {
    this._hass = hass;
    this._render();
  }

  _render() {
    // Build shadow DOM HTML
  }

  getCardSize() { return 3; }
}

customElements.define("my-card", MyCard);
```

## 8) Testing across devices

When testing custom cards, verify on all three target platforms:

| Feature | Desktop | iOS App | Android / UniFi |
|---|---|---|---|
| Button taps | click | touch→click | touch→click |
| Text input focus + keyboard | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Select/dropdown | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Date picker | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Scroll without triggering actions | mouse wheel | swipe | swipe |
| Relay service calls | always works | works if non-admin safe | works if non-admin safe |

Items marked "fragile" on Android/UniFi are sensitive to `preventDefault()` — follow §2 strictly.
